Sorry, I also just noticed that
in the LEA package description of the .geno format, they state that genotypes are coded per each individual with a single number (0/1/2/9), where
0 = zero copies of reference allele --> I guess that means homozygous for the SNP allele
1 = 1 copy of reference allele --> I guess that means heterozygous (1 SNP allele + 1 ref allele)
2 = 2 copies of reference allele --> I guess that means homozygous for the ref allele
9 = missing data

when I convert my own genelight data object into .geno with gl2geno, I get the same coding as for my original genlight object (as.matrix( my_genlight)).
For example:
genlight object:
for SNP/locus # 2, I get: 1,1,2,0,1,2,1,2 (for the first 8 individuals = first 8 rows),
.geno file:
for SNP/locus #2 (=2nd column), I get: 1,1,2,0,1,2,1,2.
I checked also other SNPs columns and the values correspond...but as far as I understood in the genlight object: ref homozygous = 0, heterozygous = 1, and SNP homozygous = 2...isn't that correct?

So...again...I am quite confused as to whether this .geno file obtained with gl2geno is incorrect OR whether it is the description in the LEA package that is incorrect...
Could you please help me understand?
Are there any other resources I can have a look at to understand genetic format types? I tried this one, https://www.cog-genomics.org/plink/1.9/formats#gen, but the .geno file does not seem to be listed/described there.

Thanks a lot!
Gabriella

